{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>The scepter shall not depart from Judah.</b> Once he holds the kingship — as he said, <b>and as a lion, who shall rouse him?</b> — the scepter of kingship shall not depart from him to any other tribe of Israel, as it says: וְחַסְדִּי לֹא יָסוּר מִמֶּנּוּ כַּאֲשֶׁר הֲסִרֹתִי מֵעִם שָׁאוּל אֲשֶׁר הֲסִרֹתִי מִלְּפָנֶיךָ (II Samuel 7:15). But when the kingship fell into the hands of the nations, that was not a removal whereby a thing passes from one place to another; rather it was destroyed entirely — for the tribe of Israel and its kingship did not depart from this one to that one, but perished utterly, and removal does not apply to what has no existence at all." "<b>And the lawgiver from between his feet.</b> That from his seed there shall be a judge seated on the throne, and between his feet shall be the scribes of the judges, according to their custom." "<b>Until Shiloh come.</b> The word שִׁילֹה is compounded from the root שׁוּל, indicating שׁוּלַיִם (end), and from the root שָׁלָה, indicating שָׁלוֹם (peace) — meaning peace of the end, i.e., the final peace. He said that what he stated — that Judah shall have scepter and lawgiver among his brothers alone, while their tribal identity endures — shall hold true until Shiloh comes." "<b>And to him shall the obedience of the peoples be.</b> But when the Messiah is revealed — he who brings peace of the end — to him shall also be the obedience of the peoples: the submissiveness of the peoples, as in אִם קֵהָה הַבַּרְזֶל (Ecclesiastes 10:10) — meaning the remnants of those who are left weakened after the destruction of their kingdoms shall heed that Shiloh he mentioned, as it says: וְקַרְקַר כָּל בְּנֵי שֵׁת (Numbers 24:17)."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:35:22.111849837Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}